#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Concern: the mechanical gates every commit clears locally — annotations, then the comment budget | Non-concern: the semantic review, attested in the message | IO: (tree) -> pass/fail
# Enable per clone: git config core.hooksPath .githooks   Bypass: git commit --no-verify
set -euo pipefail

fail() {
	echo "" >&2
	echo "pre-commit: $1 (see above)" >&2
	exit 1
}

if command -v annotated-tree >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	annotated-tree --strict-check . --hidden --max-length 200 ||
		fail "ANNOTATION GATE FAILED — a file lacks a conforming first-line annotation"
	annotated-tree --strict-check . --hidden --include-tests --max-length 200 ||
		fail "ANNOTATION GATE FAILED — a file under tests/ lacks one"
fi

# The tool gates its own commits, built from this tree so the gate grades what is being
# committed. Blocking comes from `level = "deny"` in the config, never from -W.
cargo run --quiet --release -- . 1>&2 || {
	echo "" >&2
	echo "pre-commit: COMMENT GATE FAILED — what this gate does and does not measure:" >&2
	cat >&2 <<'STANDARD'
It measures size, never quality: a comment-to-code character ratio, the length of any single
comment, and the length of any document. It cannot tell a good comment from a bad one and
does not try.

Tripping it is NOT an instruction to delete comments until the number passes. It asks why
this function needed a block of explanation, or why this document grew so long; the usual
answer is that the code is wrong, not the comment. Fix the shape: extract it, rename it,
split it.

Never raise a threshold to make a file pass. The bound is the detector, and a bigger number
only hides what it found. Where an exception is genuinely right, add an [[allow]] entry with
a reason a reviewer can check — see docs/budget-policy.md.

The bar a comment must clear to earn its space: git agent-verdict --standards programming
STANDARD
	exit 1
}
